The next step is a validation step:
\[\pi_x(\theta) = P_\theta(R(X, \theta) \le R(x, \theta)).\]
The code below finds the contour function \(\pi_x(\theta)\) given observation \(x\) and the parameter of interest \(\theta\).

get.suff.stat &lt;- function(x) {

  xbar.i &lt;- rowMeans(x)
  xbar &lt;- mean(x)
  out &lt;- c(sum((x - xbar.i)**2), sum((xbar.i)**2))
  return(out)

}

# marginal loglikelihood without the latent variable
logrellik &lt;- function(x, sig2, tau2){
  m &lt;- nrow(x)
  n &lt;- ncol(x)
  S &lt;- get.suff.stat(x)
  sig2_hat &lt;- S[1]/(m*(n-1))
  tau2_hat &lt;- (n*S[2]/m - S[1]/(m*(n-1)))/n

  f &lt;- function(u) {

    s2 &lt;- exp(u[1])
    t2 &lt;- exp(u[2])
    o1 &lt;- m * (n - 1) * log(s2) + m * log(s2 + n * t2)
    o2 &lt;- S[1] / s2 + n * S[2] / (s2 + n * t2)
    return( -(o1 + o2) / 2 )

  }

  out &lt;- list( logrellik = f(c(sig2, tau2)) - f(c(sig2_hat, tau2_hat)))
  return(out)
}

The next step is a validation step:
\[\pi_x(\theta) = P_\theta(R(X, \theta) \le R(x, \theta)).\]
For the Binomial distribution, there is a closed form for the contour.
\[\pi_x(\theta) = \sum_{y=0}^n 1 \{ R(y, \theta) \le R(x, \theta) \} p_\theta(y).\]
The code below finds the contour function \(\pi_x(\theta)\) given observation \(x\) and the parameter of interest \(\theta\).

R_binomial &lt;- function(x, n, theta){

  R &lt;- (n*theta/x)^x * (n*(1-theta)/(n-x))^(n-x)
  return(R)

}

# closed form for x=n
contour_binomial &lt;- function(theta, n, x){

  RHS &lt;- R_binomial(x, n, theta)
  total &lt;- 0
  for (i in 0:n){
    total &lt;- total + (1*(R_binomial(i, n, theta) &lt;= RHS)) * dbinom(i, n, theta)
  }
  return(total)

}
